artifact.self_contained_problem:
Catalytic computation refers to a model in which a machine has a small amount of clean workspace together with a much larger amount of "full" auxiliary memory; the machine may read and write to the auxiliary memory freely during the computation, but is required to restore the auxiliary memory to its exact initial contents at the end of the computation (regardless of what those initial contents were). Classical catalytic logspace (CL) is the class of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine using O(log n) clean workspace together with a polynomial-size catalytic tape that must be restored to its initial configuration.

Formulate the "right" quantum analogue of catalytic space. Concretely: propose a definition of a quantum catalytic computation model in which a quantum machine has access to O(log n) (or, more generally, s(n)) clean qubits of workspace and an additional polynomial-size (or 2^{O(s(n))}-size) quantum register that is initialized in an arbitrary, possibly entangled, quantum state ρ, and that must be returned exactly to ρ at the end of the computation and left uncorrelated with the workspace. Specify:

(a) whether the restoration condition should be required for all input states ρ, on average, exactly, or up to negligible trace distance;
(b) whether the catalytic register may become entangled with the workspace during the computation;
(c) whether the resulting class is robust under natural variations (Turing machine vs. uniform quantum circuit definitions, exact vs. approximate restoration, pure vs. mixed catalytic states);
(d) the relationship of the resulting class (call it QCL for quantum catalytic logspace) to standard classes such as BQL, BQP, BPL, CL, TC^1, and quantum logspace.

Determine the definition (or family of definitions) that yields a robust, well-behaved complexity class capturing the intuitive notion of "reusable quantum memory," and establish its basic inclusions and equivalences.

